  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nThe next system will impact the Ohio Vally late Wednesday into \nThursday. ILN will be placed in the warm sector on Wednesday with a \nlow pressure system that will be tracking along the northern Great \nLakes region. We will observe a bit of an increase in temperature and\nhumidity levels on Wednesday compared to today. \n\nThe biggest uncertainty for any severe potential will be based on\ntiming. Some CAMs try to usher in convection into portions of \nMercer/Auglaize Counties around 7-8 PM, which is just prior to an \ninversion developing at the surface. With modest CAPE values near \n1500-2000 J/kg and Effective Shear of 30-35 kts during that period, \nsome of these storms could be strong to severe. Primary threat would \nbe damaging straight-line winds, with large hail being a secondary \nthreat. This severe threat will likely only linger for a few of our \nnorthwestern counties (primarily in the Miami Valley, perhaps some of\nSE IN) before storms weaken as they progress southeast overnight. \nShowers/storms are expected to become more scattered to isolated \nthrough the remainder of the night. \n\nDepending on the progression of the cold front, some showers/storms\nwill remain possible for areas along and SE of the I-71 corridor on\nThursday. However, the strongest storms are likely to remain east of\nour CWA.",
